{
  "dom_id": "question-128236",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Find the value of $k$ such that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at the point where $x=0$ is equal to $0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $k\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+4x+k+8}{{({x}^{2}+4x+k)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =\\frac{-{0}^{2}+4\\cdot 0+k+8}{{({0}^{2}+4\\cdot 0+k)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{k+8}{{k}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve when $x=0$ equals $\\frac{k+8}{{k}^{2}}.$\n\nFinally, using the fact that  the slope must be equal to $0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k+8}{{k}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ k+8 & =0 \\\\ k & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-8.$",
